al node in the geopolitical landscape of Central Africa, Malabo embodies the complexities of colonial legacies and post-colonial developments. The city’s establishment coincided with European imperial ambitions in Africa, particularly by Spain, which sought to expand its influence in the region and exploit its resources. Malabo’s strategic position along maritime routes has made it a focal point for trade, migration, and cultural exchange, thus influencing the historical trajectory of the Gulf of Guinea.

